WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.520
First of all I wanted to feel reasonably confident that I knew what I was talking about.

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:10.180
In private,  I've liked learning and talking about physics ever since I was a student.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:12.620
In fact, talking about the subject

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:18.090
as soon as one has a general idea of the notions and the language is a great way to learn things.

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:24.680
Since more experienced people can then correct your misconceptions, and you really learn through that process.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:32.400
But referring to physics in my research or organizing public activities surrounding physics is

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:38.000
claiming a good deal more competence than might be required for conversations, so that took longer.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:44.580
The fact that the number theory community tends to be a pretty tough minded crowd

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:48.780
was probably part of the reason for my silence.

00:00:48.789 --> 00:00:54.449
They're not easily impressed by ideas unless you show that you can do something definite with them.

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:59.100
So I needed to have theorems, results and pretty concrete directions

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:03.059
before admitting that physics was an important part of my thinking.

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:08.489
I sometimes like to compare theorems and definite results to money in the bank.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:13.200
Then when you're just describing ideas and general philosophy

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.860
you're using that money, maybe even going into debt.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:18.800
so then you need to get back to proving theorems.

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:24.980
I should stress that I think this kind of skepticism on the part of the community is a good thing overall

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:33.959
Meeting the challenge of skeptics is definitely an important part of organizing one's thoughts thereby making them more precise and useful

00:01:34.329 --> 00:01:36.329
even if it's difficult sometimes.

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:40.420
Perhaps a more weighty aspect of the decision

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:46.600
to admit the influence of physics openly now is a certain sense of scientific obligation.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:49.940
To put it in slightly grandiose terms,

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:53.540
it's clear we're at a place in scientific history where

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:58.920
the interplay of ideas between physics and number theory is quite important and

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:03.460
young people, especially energetic young people, should be paying attention to it.

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:06.700
There are many indications,

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:12.440
such as the work on motives and Feynman diagrams, all the new work on conformal field theory

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:19.820
and modular forms, arithmetic aspects of Chern-Simons theory, S-duality, and the Langlands program;

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:21.040
there are many, many others.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:25.900
I feel strongly that the interface has

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:29.680
important insight to offer to people working in arithmetic geometry

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:34.720
but also to people working in fundamental physics especially quantum field theory and strings.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:40.569
Even if I personally say some foolish things while advertising the connections

00:02:40.569 --> 00:02:46.359
I feel like I shouldn't be hiding from a serious discourse, in private as well as in public.

